Scott asks for an approximable map \(\mathrm{comp}:(D_1\to D_2)\times(D_0\to D_1)\to(D_0\to D_2)\) with \(|\mathrm{comp}|(g,f)=g\circ f\), proved both by writing the neighbourhood relation and by building the map from evaluation and curry along his bracket sequence. `compApp` is the uncurried map sending \(\langle\langle g,f\rangle,x\rangle\) to \(g(f(x))\), defined as the outer evaluation composed with the pairing of the first projection of the function-space pair and the inner evaluation of the pairing of the second projection with the remaining argument, which places the construction on \(((D_1\to D_2)\times(D_0\to D_1))\times D_0\to D_2\). `compMap` is the currying of that map into \(D_0\to D_2\). `toElementMap_compApp` unfolds composition, pairing, the two projections, the two components of a pair, and the evaluation identity. `toElementMap_compMap_apply` rewrites by the curry computation and that elementwise identity, so the value of the composition map at a pair of maps, applied to an element, is the outer map applied to the inner map applied to the element. `toApproxMap_compMap` concludes that this value, read back as an approximable map, is the composition of the two maps, because the two sides agree on elements by the element map of a composite. `example_322` is the conjunction of those two statements. The direct neighbourhood relation for composition is not written down. **Theorem:** yes — `compMap` is the required approximable composition, and its elementwise value is \(g\circ f\). **Proof:** partial — the construction from evaluation, pairing, projections, and curry, and the element computation, are proved; the direct neighbourhood-relation proof is not given.
